{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The shoulder (glenohumeral) joint is a ball and socket joint between the head of the humerus and the shallow glenoid cavity of the scapula. It is the most mobile joint in the body, but the large head and small socket make it unstable. Stability comes mainly from the rotator cuff muscles, with help from the labrum, capsule and ligaments.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN10.12" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/shoulder-joint/shoulder-joint-anterior-overview.webp" alt="Labelled drawing of the right shoulder from the front showing the humerus, scapula, clavicle, joint capsule and surrounding ligaments." caption="The shoulder joint seen from the front, with the capsule and the ligaments that surround it." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobo_1909_193.png" width="960" height="793" /%}

## What kind of joint is the shoulder?

The glenohumeral joint is a **ball and socket joint**. The "ball" is the head of the humerus. The "socket" is the glenoid cavity of the scapula. The ball is big and the socket is small and shallow, so only about one third of the humeral head touches the glenoid at any one time. This design has been compared to a golf ball on a golf tee.

The shoulder allows a range of swing and spin wider than any other joint in the body. The price is stability. Compare the **hip**, which is stable but limited in movement.

Most of the movement happens at the glenohumeral joint, but the scapulothoracic articulation (between the scapula and the chest wall) adds to it in every direction. The whole shoulder complex includes:

- The **glenohumeral joint**
- The **acromioclavicular (AC) joint**, a fibrocartilaginous joint between the lateral end of the clavicle and the acromion, which helps stabilise the shoulder
- The **sternoclavicular joint**, a synovial joint that allows extra mobility, particularly in abduction
- The **scapulothoracic "joint"** and the **subacromial space**, which are gliding planes rather than true joints

## Bones and articular surfaces

- **Head of the humerus:** about one third of a sphere, much larger than the glenoid cavity. It faces medially, upwards and slightly backwards. The glenoid meets only the lower half of the head when the arm is in the anatomical position. The upper part of the head lies under the acromion.
- **Anatomical neck:** the slight constriction around the articular surface. The capsule attaches along its lower edge. Fractures here are uncommon.
- **Surgical neck:** the narrow area below the tubercles, where the head joins the shaft. Fractures here are common. The axillary nerve and the circumflex humeral vessels lie close to it.
- **Greater tubercle:** on the lateral side. It is the most lateral bony point of the shoulder region. The rotator cuff tendons attach to it.
- **Lesser tubercle:** on the front. The intertubercular (bicipital) groove separates it from the greater tubercle.
- **Glenoid cavity:** shallow and pear shaped. The **glenoid labrum**, a fibrocartilaginous rim around its margin, deepens the cavity and increases the contact area.

## Capsule, labrum and synovial membrane

The capsule is **lax**, which allows freedom of movement. It is attached to the lower edge of the anatomical neck of the humerus. It is **least supported inferiorly** and is under the greatest strain in full abduction, when it is stretched tightly across the humeral head.

When the arm is abducted, the lower surface of the humeral head is supported only by the long head of triceps. That muscle bows downward because of its length and gives little real support.

There are **two openings** in the capsule:

- One **below the coracoid process**, joining the joint cavity to the subscapularis bursa.
- One **between the humeral tubercles**, letting the long tendon of biceps and its synovial sheath pass out of the joint.

The **synovial membrane** lines the capsule and covers parts of the anatomical neck. The **tendon of the long head of biceps** arises from the supraglenoid tubercle and passes through the joint inside a synovial sheath. The tendon is continuous with the labrum. It leaves the joint under the transverse humeral ligament and runs in the intertubercular groove.

## Ligaments

{% table %}
- **Ligament**
- **Attachments and key points**
- **What it does**
---
- **Superior glenohumeral**
- From the supraglenoid tubercle, just in front of the origin of the long head of biceps, to the humerus near the lesser tubercle. Forms an anterior cover around the long head of biceps.
- With the coracohumeral ligament, stabilises the joint in the inferior direction and helps keep the head suspended
---
- **Middle glenohumeral**
- Arises from the anterior glenoid margin below the superior ligament and runs obliquely downward and laterally, widening as it goes
- Reinforces the capsule in front
---
- **Inferior glenohumeral**
- Has a thickened anterior band and a thinner posterior band. The thin capsule between them is the axillary pouch. It is the most important of the glenohumeral ligaments.
- Acts like a hammock that supports the humeral head in abduction
---
- **Coracohumeral**
- From the base of the coracoid process, as two bands that blend with the capsule and reach the greater and lesser tubercles. Forms a tunnel for the biceps tendon.
- Tightens in external rotation; helps suspend the head
---
- **Transverse humeral**
- A broad band between the two tubercles, attached above the epiphysial line
- Turns the intertubercular groove into a canal and holds the biceps tendon in place
---
- **Coracoacromial**
- Joins the coracoid process to the acromion
- Forms the roof (coracoacromial arch) under which the rotator cuff passes
{% /table %}

The three glenohumeral ligaments can only be seen from inside the joint. A ligament restricts movement only at the limit of a motion, when it acts as a "check-rein". Within the normal range it has no mechanical role. The capsule, ligaments and labrum contain nerve endings that sense position, and so they help the rotator cuff to stabilise the joint.

## Bursae

- **Subacromial (subdeltoid) bursa:** lies between the acromion and coracoacromial ligament above and the supraspinatus tendon below. It extends laterally under deltoid and lets the cuff glide smoothly. The subacromial and subdeltoid parts are usually continuous.
- **Subscapularis bursa:** communicates with the joint cavity.
- **Infraspinatus bursa:** may communicate with the joint cavity.

## Relations of the joint

- **Superiorly:** supraspinatus, subacromial bursa, coracoacromial ligament and deltoid
- **Inferiorly:** long head of triceps, axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral vessels
- **Anteriorly:** subscapularis, with the axillary vessels and brachial plexus further forward
- **Posteriorly:** infraspinatus and teres minor
- **Within the joint:** tendon of the long head of biceps

## The rotator cuff

The **rotator cuff** is made of four muscles, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis, and their tendons, which blend into one cuff around the joint. The cuff passes under the coracoacromial arch. The subacromial bursa lies between the two. During abduction the cuff slides outward under the arch. The cuff is the main stabiliser of the joint and also adds power to movement.

{% table %}
- **Muscle**
- **Insertion on humerus**
- **Nerve supply**
- **Main action**
---
- **Supraspinatus**
- Upper facet of the greater tubercle
- Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6)
- Starts abduction and holds the head in the glenoid
---
- **Infraspinatus**
- Middle facet of the greater tubercle
- Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6)
- Lateral rotation; draws the head into the socket
---
- **Teres minor**
- Lowest facet of the greater tubercle
- Axillary nerve (C5, C6)
- Lateral rotation
---
- **Subscapularis**
- Lesser tubercle
- Upper and lower subscapular nerves
- Medial rotation; restrains forward movement of the head
{% /table %}

The cuff has two jobs. Each muscle gives a rotational pull on the humerus. All four together **pull the head firmly into the glenoid** when deltoid contracts to abduct the arm. Deltoid and the four cuff muscles form a **force couple** that allows true abduction in the plane of the scapula.

## Movements and muscles

{% table %}
- **Movement**
- **Principal muscles**
- **Notes**
---
- **Flexion**
- Anterior fibres of deltoid, pectoralis major, biceps, coracobrachialis
- Overhead flexion also needs rotation of the scapula
---
- **Extension**
- Posterior fibres of deltoid, latissimus dorsi, teres major
- Normal range about 45 degrees
---
- **Abduction**
- Middle fibres of deltoid, helped by supraspinatus
- Supraspinatus starts the movement; abduction also needs scapular rotation
---
- **Adduction**
- Pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi, teres major, teres minor
- The arm can swing about 45 degrees across the chest
---
- **Lateral rotation**
- Infraspinatus, teres minor, posterior fibres of deltoid
- Normal range about 40 to 45 degrees
---
- **Medial rotation**
- Subscapularis, latissimus dorsi, teres major, anterior fibres of deltoid
- Normal range about 55 degrees
---
- **Circumduction**
- A combination of all the above
- The hand moves along a circle
{% /table %}

The range of any movement depends on how much free articular surface there is on the humeral head.

## Scapulohumeral rhythm

Overhead movements of flexion and abduction need smooth, coordinated movement at the glenohumeral, sternoclavicular, acromioclavicular and scapulothoracic joints. The glenohumeral joint alone cannot produce the full 170 to 180 degrees of overhead movement. The scapula rotates upward by 50 to 60 degrees to provide the rest.

The overall ratio is **2 degrees at the shoulder joint to 1 degree of scapular movement**. This is called **scapulohumeral rhythm**. For every 15 degrees of elevation, 10 occur at the glenohumeral joint and 5 by the scapula.

Other points to know:

- At about 120 degrees of abduction the greater tubercle touches the lateral edge of the acromion. Going higher needs the scapula to rotate further.
- Around 90 degrees of abduction the humeral head rotates laterally, which helps the greater tubercle clear the acromion.
- **Serratus anterior** and **trapezius** work together to turn the glenoid cavity upward, which increases the range of abduction. If serratus anterior is paralysed, the scapula "winges" and the arm cannot be abducted beyond 90 degrees.
- The scapula and upper limb hang from the clavicle by the strong coracoclavicular ligament, helped by muscle tone.

## What keeps the shoulder stable?

The shallow glenoid and the large humeral head allow a huge range of movement but make stability a problem. Stability comes from:

1. **The rotator cuff muscles:** the most important factor. The strength of the joint depends largely on the tone of subscapularis in front, supraspinatus above, and infraspinatus and teres minor behind. In short, small muscles give stability and large muscles give mobility.
2. **The glenoid labrum:** it deepens the socket.
3. **The capsule and glenohumeral ligaments:** static stabilisers. The inferior glenohumeral ligament is the most important.
4. **The long head of biceps**, which is described as an "honorary" member of the cuff.

The weak point is the **lower part of the capsule**, which has little support.

## Blood supply

The joint is supplied by branches of the **anterior and posterior circumflex humeral**, **suprascapular** and **circumflex scapular** vessels.

The shoulder is so mobile that the axillary artery can kink and be temporarily blocked. To make up for this, there is an arterial **anastomosis around the shoulder** between branches of the subclavian artery and the axillary artery. It ensures good blood flow into the limb whatever the position of the arm.

## Nerve supply

The joint is innervated mainly from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus through the subscapular nerves. The capsule is supplied by:

- The **suprascapular nerve** (posterior and superior parts)
- The **axillary nerve** (anteroinferior part)
- The **lateral pectoral nerve** (anterosuperior part, including the rotator interval)

The axillary and suprascapular nerves are the two most often named.

**Axillary nerve:** arises from the posterior cord (C5, C6). It runs along subscapularis, curves under the inferior capsule, and passes with the posterior circumflex humeral vessels through the **quadrangular space**. There it divides into anterior and posterior branches. It supplies teres minor and deltoid, and a small patch of skin over deltoid.

**Suprascapular nerve:** a branch of the upper trunk (C5, C6). It passes through the suprascapular notch below the superior transverse scapular ligament, supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus, and gives articular branches to the shoulder and acromioclavicular joints.

## Applied anatomy: dislocation

Of the large joints, the shoulder is the one that most often dislocates. The reasons are:

1. The shallow glenoid socket
2. The extraordinary range of movement
3. Underlying laxity of ligaments or abnormal glenoid development
4. The vulnerability of the joint in stressful activity of the upper limb

**Anterior dislocation:**

- It usually follows a **fall on the hand**, often with the arm abducted. The head is driven forward, tears the capsule and strips the glenoid labrum (the **Bankart lesion**).
- Sometimes the back of the head is crushed, giving a **Hill-Sachs lesion**.
- With the arm abducted, the head presses on the lower, unsupported part of the capsule. So the dislocation is almost always **subglenoid** first. The head then usually moves to a subcoracoid position.
- The **axillary nerve** lies close to the lower capsule and is endangered.
- The normal round contour of the shoulder is lost, because the greater tubercle moves medially and deltoid drops straight down. Because the shoulder is flattened, a ruler can lie on the lateral side of the arm touching the acromion and the lateral condyle of the humerus at the same time.
- Diagnosis is confirmed on an anteroposterior X-ray; an axillary view is sometimes needed.

**Posterior dislocation** usually follows a convulsion. It gives few signs and is often missed, even on X-ray. Loss of external rotation is a clue.

**Treatment:** reduction under sedation or general anaesthesia. Methods include Kocher's manoeuvre (traction, external rotation, adduction, then internal rotation), the Hippocratic method (traction with countertraction), and Stimson's technique (patient prone with the arm hanging). One source advises against Kocher's method because of the risk of nerve, vessel and bone injury. After reduction, take an X-ray, feel the pulse, and test the axillary nerve and other nerves of the arm.

## Applied anatomy: rotator cuff disease

Cuff lesions are a common cause of shoulder pain. They occur through wear (age related) or tear (repeated overhead use, as in swimmers, tennis players and throwers).

- **Supraspinatus** is the most commonly injured cuff muscle. In abduction its tendon rubs against the acromion. The subacromial bursa normally reduces this friction. Damage to the bursa is followed by damage to the tendon beneath it.
- This causes **impingement**, with a **painful arc** between about 60 and 120 degrees of abduction. Pain at 170 to 180 degrees suggests the acromioclavicular joint.
- The part of the cuff that glides between the humeral head and the acromion has a **poor blood supply**. Tears are more common in the elderly and do not heal on their own. They usually begin at the front edge of supraspinatus and spread backward to infraspinatus and teres minor.
- Impingement is most common in people over 40.
- Treatment is non-operative at first, with injections and cuff rehabilitation. Surgery is considered if symptoms last beyond 3 to 6 months. Large acute traumatic tears are best repaired early.
- Arthroscopy is the best way to diagnose superior labrum anterior and posterior (**SLAP**) tears.

## Applied anatomy: axillary nerve injury

The axillary nerve is most often injured in **shoulder dislocation** or **fracture of the surgical neck of the humerus**. The patient complains of shoulder weakness, and **deltoid is wasted**. Abduction can be started by supraspinatus but **cannot be maintained**. There is a small patch of numbness over the shoulder.

## Applied anatomy: frozen shoulder

In frozen shoulder the two layers of the synovial membrane stick together. It usually affects people aged 40 to 60. There is increasing pain and stiffness, with restriction of all movements, especially **external rotation, abduction and medial rotation**. As the glenohumeral contribution falls, the patient relies on the scapula, so scapulohumeral rhythm is altered. The disease is self-limiting and may recover in about two years, earlier with physiotherapy.

If a patient can raise both arms above the head and bring the palms together, shoulder joint disease is unlikely and deltoid and the axillary nerve are probably intact.

## Applied anatomy: referred pain and aspiration

**Shoulder tip pain:** irritation of the peritoneum under the diaphragm causes pain at the shoulder tip. The phrenic nerve (which carries sensation from that peritoneum) and the supraclavicular nerves (which supply the skin over the shoulder) both arise from spinal segments C3 and C4.

**Aspiration:** a needle can enter the joint from the front through the deltopectoral triangle, or from the side just below the acromion.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Stability versus movement:** "Small muscles give stability, large muscles give mobility." The four small cuff muscles hold the head in the socket, so that deltoid can move the arm.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Scapulohumeral rhythm:** think "2 to 1". For every 15 degrees of arm elevation, 10 happen at the shoulder joint and 5 by the scapula.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Supraspinatus starts abduction and holds the head of the humerus against the glenoid. This lets deltoid then contract and abduct the arm at the joint." %}
The main role of supraspinatus in abduction of the shoulder is to:
- A. Rotate the scapula upward
- B. Start abduction and hold the humeral head against the glenoid
- C. Rotate the humerus medially
- D. Extend the shoulder
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The lower part of the capsule is the weakest and least supported area. When the arm is abducted, the head presses on it, and the long head of triceps gives little real support. So the head usually dislocates downward first (subglenoid)." %}
Dislocation of the shoulder is usually first subglenoid mainly because:
- A. The glenoid is deepest inferiorly
- B. The labrum is thickest inferiorly
- C. The inferior capsule is lax and poorly supported, especially in abduction
- D. Supraspinatus is weak
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The overall ratio is 2 degrees at the glenohumeral joint to 1 degree of scapular movement, so 10 of every 15 degrees occur at the shoulder joint and 5 at the scapulothoracic articulation." %}
In scapulohumeral rhythm, for every 15 degrees of elevation of the arm:
- A. 10 degrees occur at the shoulder joint and 5 degrees by scapular movement
- B. 5 degrees occur at the shoulder joint and 10 degrees by scapular movement
- C. All 15 degrees occur at the shoulder joint
- D. 7.5 degrees occur at each
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="In axillary nerve injury deltoid is wasted. Supraspinatus can still start abduction, but abduction cannot be maintained. The nerve is injured in shoulder dislocation and in fractures of the surgical neck." %}
Which finding fits an axillary nerve injury after shoulder dislocation?
- A. Complete inability to start abduction
- B. Wasting of supraspinatus and infraspinatus
- C. Winging of the scapula
- D. Wasted deltoid, with abduction that can be started but not maintained
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The phrenic nerve, which carries sensation from the peritoneum under the diaphragm, and the supraclavicular nerves, which supply the skin over the shoulder, both arise from C3 and C4. So diaphragmatic irritation is felt at the shoulder tip." %}
Pain from the peritoneum under the diaphragm is felt at the shoulder tip because the phrenic and supraclavicular nerves arise from:
- A. C1 and C2
- B. C3 and C4
- C. C5 and C6
- D. C7 and C8
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="In anterior dislocation the head is driven forward, tearing the capsule and stripping the glenoid labrum. This is the Bankart lesion. A Hill-Sachs lesion is a crush of the back of the humeral head." %}
Avulsion of the glenoid labrum in anterior dislocation of the shoulder is called:
- A. Bankart lesion
- B. Hill-Sachs lesion
- C. SLAP tear
- D. Luxatio erecta
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What type of joint is the shoulder joint?" %}
A ball and socket joint between the head of the humerus and the glenoid cavity of the scapula. It is the most mobile joint in the body but is inherently unstable.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is the shoulder joint unstable?" %}
The humeral head is large and the glenoid is small and shallow, so only about one third of the head touches the socket at a time. The capsule is lax and the ligaments are weak. Stability depends mainly on the rotator cuff muscles.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the rotator cuff muscles and their nerve supply." %}
Supraspinatus and infraspinatus (suprascapular nerve), teres minor (axillary nerve), and subscapularis (upper and lower subscapular nerves).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is scapulohumeral rhythm?" %}
The coordinated movement of the shoulder joint and scapula in overhead movements, in a ratio of 2 degrees at the joint to 1 degree of scapular rotation. The scapula contributes about 50 to 60 degrees of upward rotation.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which is the weakest part of the capsule, and why does it matter?" %}
The inferior part. It is least supported and is stretched tightly in full abduction. The head dislocates through it, and the axillary nerve lies close to it.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What passes through the joint, and how does it leave?" %}
The tendon of the long head of biceps, inside a synovial sheath. It leaves under the transverse humeral ligament and runs in the intertubercular groove.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the clinical features of frozen shoulder." %}
It occurs at 40 to 60 years of age, with progressive pain and stiffness. All movements are restricted, especially external rotation, abduction and medial rotation. The synovial layers stick together, and it is self-limiting over about two years.
{% /viva %}

